==English==
{{was wotd|2008|January|21}}

===Etymology===
[[Image:Bastion (PSF).jpg|thumb|A bastion (1)]]
{{wikipedia}}
First attested in 1562. From {{der|en|fr|bastion}}, from {{der|en|fro|-}} {{m|fr|bastille||fortress}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈbæsti.ən/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈbæsti.ən/|/ˈbæstʃən/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-bastion.ogg|Audio (US)}}, {{audio|en|en-us-bastion-2.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-bastion.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{homophones|en|Bastian}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|architecture}} A [[projecting]] part of a [[rampart]] or other [[fortification]].
#* '''1942''', {{w|Emily Carr}}, ''The Book of Small'', "Beginnings," [http://www.gutenberg.net.au/ebooks04/0400201.txt]
#*: {{...}} Fort Camosun had swelled herself from being a little Hudson's Bay Fort, inside a stockade with '''bastions''' at the corners, into being the little town of Victoria, and the capital of British Columbia.
# A well-[[fortified]] [[position]]; a [[stronghold]] or [[citadel]].
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} A person, group, or thing, that strongly [[defend]]s some [[principle]].
#: {{ux|en|a '''bastion''' of hope}}
#: {{ux|en|the '''bastion''' of democracy}}

